{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The claim is broadly accurate. Indigenous peoples have resisted governments that dispossessed them of land, suppressed their cultures, imposed forced assimilation, or denied their autonomy.\n\nExamples include Māori resistance to British colonial expansion in New Zealand, Native American resistance to forced removal in the United States, and Maya resistance to state repression in Guatemala. Resistance has included armed struggle, legal challenges, protest, and cultural preservation.\n\nThe claim is a generalization: Indigenous peoples are diverse, and assessing a particular government as oppressive requires examining its policies and actions."}}
